## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design end-to-end CAPA for privacy-policy drift in a multi-tenant, partner‑integrated data plane. Requires data modeling, lifecycle management, API surfaces, observability, and rollout safety.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model including lineage and policy versions
- Region/tenant-aware metrics
- Lifecycle state machine and RCA templates
- Canary rollout and rollback safeguards

## Code Example
```javascript
type CAPA = {
  id: string;
  tenantId: string;
  region: string;
  policyVersion: string;
  evidenceRefs: string[];
  dataLineage: string;
  rootCause?: string;
  correctiveAction?: string;
  containmentStatus: 'detected'|'investigating'|'contained'|'verified'|'closed';
  timestamps: { detected: string; updated?: string; closed?: string };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the canary rollout for a policy upgrade in this scenario?
- What data access controls and retention would you enforce during CAPA investigations?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests depth in CAPA design for cross-tenant data exposure caused by policy migration bugs in a real-time data mesh. It evaluates the ability to model evidence, define a region-aware lifecycle, implement a minimal API, and build measurable containment strategies plus RCA artifacts and canary rollouts.

## Key Concepts

- CAPA data model capturing evidence, tenant IDs, policy metadata, dashboards, and timestamps
- Region-aware lifecycle state machine for CAPA progression
- RBAC/policy migration risks in real-time data platforms
- Minimal REST API design to link logs/traces to CAPA records
- Canary rollout and region-validated testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal CAPA creation endpoint
app.post('/api/capa', async (req, res) => {
  const { incidentId, tenants, dashboards, policyHash, evidence } = req.body;
  // Persist CAPA, link logs/traces, trigger workflow
  res.status(201).send({ id: incidentId });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate cross-tenant exposure detection at scale across regions?
- What data retention and privacy considerations would you encode in the CAPA API?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft a CAPA program for cross-tenant data exposure caused by authorization cache mishandling. Emphasizes data modeling, lifecycle governance, API design, observability across regions, and rollback safety.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model with evidence, containment, remediation, and audit logs
- Region-aware, tenant-scoped metrics and dashboards
- Minimal REST API design for CAPA lifecycle
- RCA templates and controlled canary rollout for remediation
- Safe rollback and rollback criteria during canary

## Code Example
```javascript
interface CAPA {
  id: string;
  incidentId: string;
  tenantId: string;
  service: string;
  resource: string;
  evidence: string[];
  containmentActions: string[];
  remediation: string;
  status: "detected" | "triaged" | "contained" | "remediated" | "verified" | "closed";
  region?: string;
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate the canary rollout criteria and rollback?
- What logging/telemetry would you require to prove no similar exposure occurs post-remediation?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests ability to design a production-grade CAPA for a multi-tenant data platform, emphasizing isolation, auditability, and scalable rollout.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped CAPA data model with evidence and artifacts
- Immutable links to logs/traces and resource metadata
- Lifecycle state machine and canary-based enforcement
- Per-tenant metrics (recurrence, MTTR, spillover rate)

## Code Example
```javascript
// TypeScript interface for CAPA
interface CAPA {
  id: string;
  tenantId: string;
  policyId: string;
  incidentId: string;
  evidenceLinks: string[];
  artifacts: string[];
  affectedResources: string[];
  dataClassification: string;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle CAPA versioning and backward compatibility?
- How to simulate cross-tenant leakage in a canary without impacting production?

## Why This Is Asked

Gauges ability to design end-to-end CAPA artifacts for data exposure in multi-tenant data platforms.

## Key Concepts

- CAPA data modeling
- Lifecycle state machines
- API design for incident records
- Telemetry and tenant-scoped metrics
- RCA templates and Canary remediation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal REST handler sketch for creating a CAPA
app.post('/caps', (req, res) => {
  const { cap_id, incident, tenant_id, evidence } = req.body;
  if (!cap_id || !tenant_id) return res.status(400).send('Missing fields');
  // store CAPA entry
  res.status(201).json({ cap_id, status: 'created' });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the CAPA under concurrent backup runs?
- What metrics would you monitor to ensure containment over time?
